ForgeAnalysisRingV1
===================

Status: frozen host-internal shared-memory layout foundation
Version: 1
Byte order: little-endian
Atomic unit: aligned unsigned 64-bit integer
Cache-line alignment: 64 bytes
Canonical payload: one complete Forge CanonicalRecordEnvelopeV1 encoded record

This contract defines one single-producer/single-consumer analysis ring. Each
consumer receives an independent ring. A slow or failed analysis consumer may
drop records on its own branch but must never wait for or backpressure the
acquisition journal. Any drop in a closed-loop controller ring latches the
controller unavailable and requires stimulation disarm. This contract does not
authorize stimulation and is never exposed to React or the WebView.

Global header: 256 bytes
------------------------

offset  size  field
0       8     magic = ASCII FGRRNG01
8       2     version = 1
10      2     global_header_bytes = 256
12      2     slot_header_bytes = 64
14      2     reserved_zero
16      4     slot_count, range 2..65536
20      4     slot_payload_capacity_bytes, range 176..1048752
24      4     static_flags = 0
28      4     reserved_zero
32      32    forge_protocol_hash
64      16    run_id, nonzero
80      16    consumer_id, nonzero
96      8     producer_epoch, nonzero
104     8     slot_stride_bytes, 64-byte aligned
112     4     CRC32C over immutable bytes [0,112)
116     12    reserved_zero
128     8     published_sequence, atomic; next sequence to publish
136     8     consumed_sequence, atomic; next sequence to consume
144     8     dropped_records, atomic
152     8     producer_heartbeat_monotonic_ns, atomic
160     8     consumer_heartbeat_monotonic_ns, atomic
168     8     fault_flags, atomic
176     80    reserved_zero

fault_flags
  bit 0  consumer ring full / record dropped
  bit 1  slot contradiction or corruption
  all other bits reserved and rejected

Slot header: 64 bytes
---------------------

offset  size  field
0       8     committed_sequence, atomic; UINT64_MAX means empty
8       4     encoded_record_bytes
12      4     encoded_record_crc32c
16      8     journal_sequence
24      8     canonical record_sequence cache
32      8     canonical global_time_start_ns cache
40      4     canonical record flags cache
44      4     reserved_zero
48      8     committed_sequence_complement
56      8     reserved_zero
64      N     complete canonical encoded record; unused capacity is zero

Publish order
-------------

1. Load consumed_sequence with acquire ordering.
2. If published_sequence - consumed_sequence >= slot_count, atomically
   increment dropped_records, set fault bit 0, reject the newest record and
   return immediately.
3. Validate the canonical record and write slot metadata, payload, CRC and
   committed_sequence_complement.
4. Store committed_sequence with release ordering.
5. Store published_sequence = sequence + 1 with release ordering.

Consume order
-------------

1. Load published_sequence with acquire ordering. Equal read/write sequences
   mean empty.
2. Load the expected slot committed_sequence with acquire ordering and require
   both it and its complement to match.
3. Check bounds, CRC32C, the complete canonical decoder and all redundant cache
   fields before exposing a SampleBlock to an algorithm.
4. Store committed_sequence = UINT64_MAX, then store consumed_sequence =
   sequence + 1 with release ordering.

Restart and security boundary
-----------------------------

A worker restart never resumes an old mapping. The daemon creates a new mapping
with a fresh producer_epoch and consumer-specific identity. A production
Windows mapping requires an explicit service/operator/worker SID DACL and an
authenticated named-pipe handoff. Byte-layout, mapping, cross-process ordering,
handoff, restart and target-load behavior require separate evidence gates.
